import gfx, shell; from shell import fig
G = gfx; F = G.F; c = F["budget"]["city"]; h = F["hourly"]
A_URL, B_URL, M_URL = "https://claude.ai/artifact/MNNFwBaYTPbEFhDqcVXky8", "https://claude.ai/artifact/PxLcv4ugwZ9xBFydpEJ4wj", "https://claude.ai/artifact/HkkFNpzoEPwdLzC7mcPLrZ"
def row(q, f, tag, cls): return f"<tr><td><b>{q}</b></td><td>{f}</td><td><span class='tag {cls}'>{tag}</span></td></tr>"
rows = "".join([
 row("Where does a bus's time go, citywide?", "Of every hour: 21 min moving, 10 at red lights, 11 at stops beside a light, 5 at other stops, 13 in other traffic. 8.3 mph against 23.7 mph on a clear run. The last published split (Comptroller, 2017) was 21% red lights, 22% stops.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("Is traffic the main problem?", "No. On Sundays other-traffic delay is 23% lower, yet buses are only 6% faster (8.7 against 8.2 mph). At 3am, with empty streets, buses still lose 39% of the delay they lose at 3pm. The slowest hour is 2 to 3pm (6.9 mph), not rush hour.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("Does a busway work? (East Tremont Av, opened 19 Sept)", "Delay inside the busway fell 32% westbound and 14% eastbound; travel time 25% and 12%. By cause: other traffic −56% westbound and −30% eastbound, stop delay −4% to −15%, lights −5% to −48%. Blocks outside the busway did not change. Of 256 half-mile stretches of Bronx bus street, the busway was the single most improved.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("What happens when cars are banned? (42nd St, World Cup match days)", "M42 about 20% faster from noon to 8pm across seven match days, against ordinary days in the same mix of weekdays, Saturdays and Sundays (an eighth, on a Monday evening, was 6% faster); the same as usual before the closures began. 34th, 50th and 57th Streets ran 3 to 11% slower over the same hours. Along the street, other-traffic delay fell 27 to 38% over the whole day and stop delay did not fall.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("Does 14th Street's busway show up against its neighbours?", "Yes. Other-traffic delay is 1.1 min per mile on 14th St against 3.4 on 34th, 42nd and 57th. Stops and lights are no better, so it is still under 6 mph.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Does signal priority work? (288 lights switched on)", "At lights with no stop in front: 0.7 s saved per bus (4%), 73% of lights improved. At lights with a near-side stop: no change. Three intersections cut the wait by about a fifth (Springfield Blvd at Jamaica Av, Watson Av at Metcalf Av, Forest Av at Fairview Av).", "Strong", "s"),
 row("Do bus-camera fines speed up buses? (22 routes, 6 start dates)", "No detectable effect: travel time +0.4% (±0.25) after fines began, against routes not yet fined. Same answer on streets served only by the newly fined routes. The MTA claims 5% on average.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Does it matter which side of the light the stop is on?", "With traffic removed, a light alone costs 11 s; with a far-side stop 23 s; with a near-side stop 31 s. There are 3,300 near-side stops at lights. Cross-sectional, so busier corners may explain part of it.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("What do turns cost?", "A left turn at a light costs 40 s against 24 s going straight; a right turn 32 s. Lights at major cross streets cost roughly 30% more than lights at side streets.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Is delay concentrated?", "Half of rush-hour lost time is on 220 of 1,950 miles of street (11%). 5% of intersections hold 25% of the intersection delay; 14% hold half.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("Does the Bus Action Plan cover the delay?", "Its 50 corridors, clipped to their limits, cover 10% of bus-street miles, 25% of rush-hour lost time and 77 of the 220 worst miles. Fifth Avenue, the street that loses the most (263 bus-hours each weekday rush), is not among them; nor are Queens Blvd, Hillside Av, Hylan Blvd or Third Av.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Do painted bus lanes help, street against street?", "Barely. Holding lanes, lights, stops and borough equal, a full-length bus lane goes with 34 s less delay per mile (about 10%). First, Second, Third and Fifth Avenues have lanes and still lose much of their time to other traffic.", "Weak", "w"),
 row("Similar streets, different delay?", "Yes. Ocean Av and Coney Island Av in Brooklyn run parallel, with the same lanes, lights, stops and bus volumes: 9.3 against 7.3 mph. First and Second Avenues: 7.7 against 6.3 mph. Each extra light per mile adds about 15 s per mile.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Did the Q70's centre lane on Broadway, Queens, work?", "Yes. Eastbound from 69th St to Roosevelt Av the Q70 went from 5.2 to 5.9 mph all day (travel time −11%) and from 4.3 to 5.1 mph from 3 to 8pm, starting the week of 1 June and holding through November. Westbound, with no lane, did not improve. Only five weeks of data come before it, and signal priority was added at the same time.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("New bus lanes on Madison (done 7 July) and Lexington?", "Madison 25th to 42nd St: about 5% faster than neighbouring blocks, but the only 'before' is a construction period. Lexington 60th to 52nd St: 0 to 6% depending on the comparison.", "Weak", "w"),
 row("Does school traffic matter? (single-day tests)", "Yes, a lot. On Election Day, with schools remote and offices open, buses ran 6% faster citywide than on other autumn Tuesdays. The first day of school was 9% slower than summer Thursdays. Veterans Day was 21% faster.", "Strong", "s"),
 row("One-off disruptions", "Rail strike (Mon 18 May): Queens buses 3% slower, 7% around Jamaica; other boroughs unchanged. Summer Streets Saturdays: avenues beside Park Av 9 to 12% slower; Grand Concourse 13% slower on its day. Knicks parade: lower Manhattan 15% slower at midday. West Indian Day Parade: streets crossing Eastern Pkwy 34% slower. Marathon, floods, the 100-degree day, gridlock alert days and the A-train shutdown: no clear effect.", "Medium", "m"),
 row("Did the busway make buses more evenly spaced?", "No change we can see. The variation in gaps between buses grows across the busway about as much as before: +0.06 eastbound both before and after, +0.02 before and +0.04 after westbound. Service was also slightly more frequent after.", "Weak", "w"),
 row("What does UN week do?", "21 to 25 Sept: buses on Lexington (60th to 52nd St) averaged 3.2 mph and on Madison (42nd to 60th St) 3.6 mph, against 5.7 and 6.0. Citywide speeds did not move.", "Strong", "s"),
])
body = f'''
<div class="col">
<p class="kicker">Research summary · New York City buses, 27 April to 21 November 2026</p>
<h1>What Seven Months of Bus Data Say About Why New York’s Buses Are Slow</h1>
<p class="dek">Nineteen questions, asked of every bus on every route. The short version: a bus spends 39 of every 60 minutes not moving freely, traffic is a third of that, and the only thing that reliably helped this year was taking road space from cars.</p>
<p class="byline">Executive summary · two pitch mock-ups are linked below · all figures are our own estimates unless a source is named</p>
<h2>The findings that matter most</h2>
<ol>
<li><b>Lights and stops, not traffic, take most of the lost time.</b> Of an average hour, 26 minutes go to red lights and stops and 13 to other traffic. Sundays, with almost a quarter less traffic delay, are only 6% faster.</li>
<li><b>Physical priority works.</b> The East Tremont busway cut delay inside it by 32% westbound. Closing 42nd Street to cars on World Cup match days made the M42 about 20% faster for the hours of the closure.</li>
<li><b>The cheap measures barely register.</b> Signal priority saved 0.7 seconds per light. Camera fines on 22 more routes changed nothing we could measure.</li>
<li><b>The Bus Action Plan misses most of the worst streets.</b> Its 50 corridors cover 77 of the 220 miles of street that hold half the rush-hour lost time. Fifth Avenue, the worst street in the city for buses, is not among them.</li>
<li><b>Stop placement is an overlooked lever.</b> A stop before the light costs 4 to 8 seconds more per bus than a stop after it, at 3,300 lights, and it switches off signal priority.</li>
</ol>
<div class="box"><h3>Two pitch mock-ups</h3>
<p><a href="{A_URL}"><b>The streets the bus plan missed.</b></a> The delay map against the plan’s 50 corridors, twelve streets it leaves out, and what each needs.</p>
<p><a href="{B_URL}"><b>What actually sped up buses.</b></a> Alternate angle: seven real-world tests from this year, ranked by what they did.</p>
<p>Method and corridor graphics: <a href="{M_URL}">Where NYC Buses Lose Time</a>.</p></div>
</div>
{fig("1. Out of every hour on a bus, 21 minutes are spent moving", "Where the time goes on the average New York City bus, all routes, all days.", G.budget(), "34,450 bus-hours a day were measured, on 2,660 directional miles of street between traffic lights. Our citywide speed (8.3 mph; 8.5 in August) matches the MTA’s published figures (8.3; 8.5).", legend=G.legend())}
{fig("2. What each change did to bus travel time", "Change on the streets where it happened, compared with similar streets over the same weeks.", G.scoreboard(), "See the second pitch for the detail behind each bar. None is a randomised trial; the busway and 42nd Street results are the cleanest.")}
{fig("3. Half of the rush-hour delay is on 220 miles of street; the plan reaches 77 of them", "Blue is inside one of the Bus Action Plan’s 50 corridors; orange is outside.", G.citymap(), "Weekday rush hours; both directions of a street counted together. Corridor limits are printed in the plan for 24 corridors and read from its map for the rest.", scroll=False, legend='<div class="legend"><span><i style="border-color:var(--c-miss)"></i>Worst-delay miles outside the plan</span><span><i style="border-color:var(--c-plan)"></i>Worst-delay miles on a plan corridor</span><span><i style="border-color:var(--c-plan);opacity:.4"></i>Rest of the plan corridors</span></div>')}
{fig("4. A stop before the light costs more than a stop after it", "Average delay around an intersection, for buses going straight through.", G.placement3(), "Only the delay credited to the light and the stop is shown; other traffic is removed. Averages over intersections with at least 350 feet of clear street before and 300 after, buses going straight through. Seconds are per 100 feet. With a far-side stop, delay before the light is counted as the light’s and delay after it as the stop’s. Stops are not placed at random, so part of the gap may reflect busier corners. Counting all time above a clear run within 250 feet, traffic included, gives the same order: 30, 39 and 43 seconds.", legend=G.legend(("lights", "stop", "near")))}
{fig("5. Two parallel Brooklyn avenues, alike on paper, two miles an hour apart", "Delay along Ocean Avenue (B49) and Coney Island Avenue (B68), half a mile apart, drawn to the same scale. Red is lights, blue is stops, orange is other traffic.", G.twins(), "Matched on lanes, lights per mile, stops per mile and buses per day. Coney Island Avenue loses almost 50% more time at lights and in traffic. We do not know why; double parking along its commercial strip is the obvious candidate.")}
{fig("6. The busway did not make the Bx36 more evenly spaced", "How uneven the gaps between buses are along East Tremont Avenue (0 is perfectly even, 1 is as uneven as random arrivals), before the busway and with it.", G.cvfig(), "Coefficient of variation of the gap between consecutive Bx36 buses at each point along the street, weekdays, all hours pooled, which inflates the level; the comparison between periods is what matters. Before is 18 May to 26 June, after is 28 September to 20 November. Lines rise in steps because unevenness grows mainly at stops and lights.", legend='<div class="legend"><span><i style="border-color:var(--fg2)"></i>Before</span><span><i style="border-color:var(--c-lane)"></i>With the busway</span></div>')}
<div class="col">
<h2>Every question we asked</h2>
</div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Question</th><th>What the data says</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div>
<div class="col">
<h2>Numbers for scale</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Riders.</b> About 1.31 million paid bus trips each weekday in 2026 (MTA daily ridership data), averaging about 2 miles (federal transit database). At 8.3 mph that is roughly 320,000 rider-hours on board a day, of which about 53,000 are at red lights with no stop and 70,000 in other traffic. These are rough conversions of ours.</li>
<li><b>Cost.</b> A bus-hour costs about $309 to run (federal transit database, 2025). The 22,400 bus-hours a day above a clear run are worth about $7 million a day at that rate. A clear run is not achievable, so treat this as a ceiling.</li>
<li><b>Near-side stops.</b> Moving all 3,300 across the intersection would save an estimated 550 to 1,150 bus-hours a day, before any gain from signal priority.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Other before-and-after tests in the window</h2>
<p>Web research turned up about thirty dated changes. Besides those above: Flatbush Avenue’s centre lanes (ribbon-cutting 17 November, only four days of data after); Fordham Road and 116th Street (still under construction);  Bay Parkway and DeKalb/Lafayette Avenues (construction began in October and November). These have not been analysed. The one-off events are in the table above.</p>
<h2>What to check before publishing</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>The plan’s corridor extents.</b> The map and street ranking now follow the method of the earlier report, “Where New York’s Buses Lose Their Time” (weekday rush hours, directions merged, corridors clipped to their limits). Our figures reproduce its totals within 1%: 13,484 against 13,441 bus-hours, Fifth Avenue 263 against 261, plan share 24.6% against 24.9%. Four corridors match its extents only roughly (116th St, Church Av, Kensington–JFK, Flatbush Av south of the park). Twenty-six corridors have no printed limits; NYC DOT should confirm them.</li>
<li><b>Fifth Avenue.</b> Checked: a busway from 57th to 34th Street was announced in June 2020 and dropped by August (Streetsblog); the street has had two bus lanes from 61st to 34th Street since 2018; the “Future of Fifth” redesign would leave one. Whether the current administration has changed that redesign was not verified. The plan’s text does not mention Fifth Avenue.</li><li><b>The 42nd Street closure</b> is confirmed by the city’s notice of 29 May 2026. We did not check independently that it was carried out on each day, though the bus speeds say it was.</li>
<li><b>Existing bus lanes</b> in the street-against-street comparison come from OpenStreetMap, which is incomplete (it shows lanes on only 14% of Fifth Avenue). For the twelve streets in the first pitch they were checked by hand; Broadway in the Bronx, Springfield Boulevard and Union Turnpike could not be verified either way.</li>
<li><b>Camera enforcement dates</b> came from MTA press-release titles and local news; two of the six dates are inferred from the 60-day warning period.</li>
<li><b>The split between lights, stops and other traffic</b> depends on where the other-traffic baseline is drawn (the lowest 5% of the delay curve within 750 feet, smoothed; everything above it goes to the nearest stop or light). Tested on 125 streets: on streets with a light every block, other traffic is overstated by up to about 15 points of the delay.</li>
<li><b>Positions are every 30 seconds</b> and there is no door data. Bus-hours count buses, not riders.</li>
</ul>
<p class="note">Sources for context figures: MTA open data (daily ridership, bus speeds); FTA National Transit Database 2025; NYC Comptroller (2017, 2025); Mayor’s Office and NYC DOT releases, 2026; “Next Stop: Fast Buses, Better Service” (July 2026); NYC emergency-management World Cup street closure notice; Streetsblog NYC.</p>
</div>
'''
open(shell.HERE / "summary.html", "w").write(shell.page("NYC Bus Delay Findings", body)); print(len(body) // 1024, "KB")
